<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1854 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Mexican National Anthem</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="es">Himno Nacional Mexicano</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Mexico's national anthem: the words won a contest in 1854 and the music was chosen the same year.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Francisco González Bocanegra</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Jaime Nunó</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1853–1854; music chosen 12 August 1854; adopted 16 September 1854</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Francisco González Bocanegra (1853), music by Jaime Nunó (1854), both published before 1923)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>At the time the anthem was written, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was still feeling the effects of its bitter defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>, in which it had ceded more than half of its territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the country was demoralised and divided. On 12 November 1853 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> announced a competition for a national anthem, with a prize for the best poem of patriotic ideals. The poet Francisco González Bocanegra at first refused to enter, saying that writing love poems needed very different skills. According to the story told of it, his fiancée, Guadalupe González del Pino, lured him into a bedroom of her parents' house, locked him in, and would not let him out until he had written an entry. Pictures of events in Mexican history in the room helped to inspire him, and after four hours he slid all ten stanzas under the door. He won the competition by unanimous vote, and his victory was announced in the Official Journal of the Federation on 3 February 1854.</p><p>A musical setting was chosen at the same time. The winner, Juan Bottesini, was rejected because his entry was disliked, and a second contest was held. It was won on 12 August 1854 by Jaime Nunó, a Catalan-born band leader whom Santa Anna had met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to direct its military bands; his music was titled &quot;Dios y libertad&quot; (&quot;God and Freedom&quot;). The song was adopted as the national anthem on Independence Day, 16 September 1854. Its first performance, at the Santa Anna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a>, was directed by Bottesini and sung by the soprano Claudia Florenti and the tenor Lorenzo Salvi.</p><p>The anthem has ten stanzas and a chorus. Since 1943, by a decree of President Manuel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> Camacho, the official anthem consists of the chorus and the first, fifth, sixth and tenth stanzas; at sporting events such as the Olympic Games and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">FIFA World Cup</a> only the chorus and first stanza are sung. Mexican law regulates its use closely: it may not be altered or used for commercial purposes, it must be performed with solemnity, those present stand at attention, and it is taught in schools and played at the start and close of radio and television broadcasting. Performers who have got it wrong in public have been fined. A long-told story that its music was privately owned by a German publisher and later by Nunó and two Americans is not correct: the anthem is in the public domain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and under Mexican law the state holds moral rights, but not property rights, to the national symbols.</p><p>The historian Javier Garciadiego, speaking at the ceremony for the anthem's 150th anniversary in 2004, said that it set aside divisions and strife and encouraged national unity; on that day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> and other parts of the country stopped to sing it together, as did Mexicans abroad. It has been described as one of the symbols of Mexican identity. An archaic line of the first stanza has often been misheard as the name of an enemy, and the misheard word has been used as a personal name and as a nickname for the anthem itself. Since a change to the law on 8 December 2005, the words may be translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s Indigenous languages; official translations exist in Chinanteco, Hña Hñu, Mixtec, Maya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and Tenek.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to defend the homeland against any foreign enemy, recalling the country's victories in battle and praising those who fight for it. Its mood is martial and solemn, and its theme is a united nation ready to defend itself.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Mexicanos, al grito de guerra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mexicans, at the cry of war</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El acero aprestad y el bridón,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make ready the steel and the steed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y retiemble en sus centros la tierra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and let the earth tremble to its centres</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Al sonoro rugir del cañón.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at the sonorous roar of the cannon.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ciña ¡oh Patria! tus sienes de oliva</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May he gird, O Homeland, your brow with olive,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De la paz el arcángel divino,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the divine archangel of peace,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Que en el cielo tu eterno destino</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for in heaven your eternal destiny</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Por el dedo de Dios se escribió;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by the finger of God was written;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Mas si osare un extraño enemigo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But if a foreign enemy should dare</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Profanar con su planta tu suelo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to profane your soil with his foot,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Piensa ¡oh Patria querida! que el cielo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>think, O beloved Homeland, that heaven</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Un soldado en cada hijo te dió.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>gave you a soldier in each son.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En sangrientos combates los viste</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In bloody combats you saw them,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Por tu amor palpitando sus senos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>their breasts beating for love of you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Arrostrar la metralla serenos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>face the grapeshot serenely</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y la muerte ó la gloria buscar.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and seek death or glory.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 84 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Si el recuerdo de antiguas hazañas</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If the memory of ancient deeds</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De tus hijos inflama la muerte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>inflames your sons' death,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Los laureles del triunfo, tu frente</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the laurels of triumph, your brow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Volverán inmortales á ornar.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>will return immortal to adorn.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Como al golpe del rayo la encina</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As, at the stroke of lightning, the oak</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Se derrumba hasta el hondo torrente,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>crashes down into the deep torrent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">La discordia vencida, impotente,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>discord, defeated, powerless,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">A los pies del arcángel cayó:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>fell at the archangel's feet:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ya no más de tus hijos la sangre</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>no more shall the blood of your sons</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Se derrame en contienda de hermanos;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>be spilled in strife of brothers;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Sólo encuentre el acero en tus manos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let him alone find the steel in your hands</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Quien tu nombre sagrado insultó.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who has insulted your sacred name.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Del guerrero inmortal de Zempoala</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of the immortal warrior of Zempoala</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Te defienda la espada terrible,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>may the terrible sword defend you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y sostenga su brazo invencible</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and may his invincible arm uphold</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Tu sagrado pendón tricolor;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your sacred tricolour banner;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Él será del feliz mexicano</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he will be, for the happy Mexican,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En la paz y en la guerra el caudillo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in peace and in war the leader,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Porque él supo sus armas de brillo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>because he knew how, with brilliance, his arms</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Circundar en los campos de honor.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to encircle on the fields of honour.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Guerra, guerra sin tregua al que intente</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>War, war without truce on him who tries</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De la patria manchar los blasones!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to stain the homeland's coats of arms!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Guerra, guerra! los patrios pendones</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>War, war! The native banners</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En las olas de sangre empapad:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>soak in the waves of blood:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Guerra, guerra! En el monte, en el valle</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>War, war! On the mountain, in the valley</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Los cañones horrísimos truenen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let the most horrid cannons thunder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y los ecos sonoros resuenen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and let the sonorous echoes resound</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Con las voces de ¡Unión! ¡Libertad!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with the cries of Union! Liberty!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Antes, Patria, que inermes tus hijos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sooner, Homeland, than your sons, unarmed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Bajo el yugo su cuello dobleguen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>should bend their necks beneath the yoke,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Tus campiñas con sangre se rieguen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let your fields be watered with blood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Sobre sangre se estampe su pie;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let their foot be stamped upon blood;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y tus templos, palacios y torres</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and let your temples, palaces and towers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Se derrumben con hórrido estruendo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>crash down with a horrid din,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y sus ruinas existan diciendo:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and their ruins remain, saying:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De mil héroes la Patria aquí fué.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Here was the Homeland of a thousand heroes.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Si á la lid contra hueste enemiga</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If to battle against an enemy host</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Nos convoca la trompa guerrera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the warlike trumpet calls us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De Iturbide la sacra bandera</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sacred flag of Iturbide,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Mexicanos! valientes seguid:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mexicans! follow valiantly:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y á los fieros bridones les sirvan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and for the fierce steeds let</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Las vencidas enseñas de alfombra;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the vanquished ensigns serve as a carpet;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Los laureles del triunfo dén sombra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let the laurels of triumph give shade</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">A la frente del bravo adalid.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to the brow of the brave commander.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Vuelva altivo á los patrios hogares</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let him return proud to the homeland's hearths,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El guerrero á contar su victoria,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the warrior, to tell of his victory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ostentando las palmas de gloria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>displaying the palms of glory</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Que supiera en la lid conquistar:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that he knew how to conquer in battle:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Tornaránse sus lauros sangrientos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>his bloody laurels will turn</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En guirnaldas de mirtos y rosas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>into garlands of myrtle and roses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Que el amor de las hijas y esposas</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for the love of daughters and wives</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">También sabe á los bravos premiar.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>also knows how to reward the brave.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y el que al golpe de ardiente metralla</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And he who, at the blast of burning grapeshot,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De la Patria en las aras sucumba,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>succumbs on the altars of the Homeland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Obtendrá en recompensa una tumba</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>will obtain in reward a tomb</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Donde brille de gloria la luz:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where the light of glory shines:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y de Yguala la enseña querida</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the beloved ensign of Iguala,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">A su espada sangrienta enlazada,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>bound to his bloody sword,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De laurel inmortal coronada,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>crowned with immortal laurel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Formará de su fosa la cruz.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>will form the cross of his grave.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Patria! ¡Patria! tus hijos te juran</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Homeland! Homeland! Your sons swear to you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Exhalar en tus aras su aliento,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to breathe their last breath on your altars,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Si el clarín con su bélico acento</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if the bugle with its warlike accent</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Los convoca a lidiar con valor:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>calls them to fight with valour:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Para ti las guirnaldas de oliva;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For you the garlands of olive!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Un recuerdo para ellos de gloria!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For them a memory of glory!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Un laurel para ti de victoria;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For you a laurel of victory!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Un sepulcro para ellos de honor!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For them a tomb of honour!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Francisco González Bocanegra (1853); the full text of 1854. Text from <a href="https://es.wikisource.org/wiki/Himno_Nacional_Mexicano_(1854)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.2: 'bridón' is a bridled horse, a war-horse.</li><li>lines 2.1-2.2: the subject is the archangel: 'may the divine archangel of peace gird your brow with olive'.</li><li>line 5.2: 'De tus hijos inflama la muerte' is obscure; literally 'inflames the death of your sons', usually understood as 'fires your sons to [face] death'.</li><li>line 7.1: the 'immortal warrior of Zempoala' is Antonio López de Santa Anna, president when the anthem was adopted; the stanza was later dropped.</li><li>line 10.3: Iturbide's flag: the Trigarante flag of Agustín de Iturbide's Army of the Three Guarantees; line 12.5, 'Yguala' (Iguala), where his Plan of Iguala was proclaimed (1821).</li><li>lines 5.3-5.4 and 7.7-7.8: kept in the Spanish order; read 'will return, immortal, to adorn your brow' and 'because he knew how to encircle his arms with brilliance on the fields of honour'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
